{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L16LAYmErsUU565t14bVDr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. It's an experiment in specification-driven development using an AI code generation pipeline.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/` - One package per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - Hash/checksum shared code\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - Size parsing\n- `pkg/encutil/` - Encoding utilities\n- `magefiles/` - Build automation (external, don't modify)\n- `docs/` - Specifications (don't modify)\n\n**Current State**: Looking at the requirement states, I can see which SRDs have requirements ready for implementation. From the data, srd004-ts has all its requirements in ready state from R1.1 through R10.3.\n\nNow I'm examining the breakdown of ready requirements across the other SRDs: srd103-pathchk has 3 ready, srd104-test has 8, srd105-stty has 11, srd106-df has 18, and srd107-dir has 9 requirements that are marked as ready.\n\nContinuing through the list, srd108-vdir shows 10 ready requirements, srd109-dircolors has 14, srd110-pr has 10, srd111-ptx has 9, and srd112-chronic has 6 requirements ready so far.\n\nNow I'm mapping each specification to its corresponding release batch and noting that most are still in the not started phase for code development.\n\nLooking at the earlier releases, I notice there are incomplete use cases in Release 00.0 and 01.1 that have finished specs but haven't begun implementation yet. However, I need to check the requirement_states constraint—since those SRDs aren't listed as having \"ready\" requirements, I shouldn't propose tasks for them regardless of their code status.\n\nFiltering by what's actually ready, the earliest release with available work is Batch 12.1, which has pathchk and test with ready requirements, though tsort also appears in that batch without any ready requirements listed. The next batch up is 13.0 with df, dir, vdir, and dircolors all showing ready requirements. Continuing through the earlier releases, I'm seeing a pattern where most use cases marked as \"not started\" don't have any ready requirements associated with them, so they won't help me move forward right now. Looking at the earliest available release, rel12.1 has pathchk and test with ready requirements, while tsort doesn't have any ready dependencies yet. I'll prioritize those two tasks first, then move to rel13.0 if I have capacity remaining. Since I can't access the requirements.yaml file directly, I'm assuming a default weight of 1 for all requirements unless the project context indicates otherwise—though I know weight-4 requirements exist in later releases. the filesystem, read files, or run commands.\" So I have to work with what's given. I'll assume default weight of 1 for requirements not explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nBut actually, I need to be careful. The step 3b says to check weights from requirements.yaml. Since I can't read that file and it's not provided in the context, I'll note my assumption and proceed with weight=1 as default.\n\nNow let me plan the tasks. I'm limited to 4 tasks max.\n\n**Release 12.1**: pathchk and test\n\n### srd103-pathchk (3 ready requirements: R2.1, R2.2, R2.3)\n- codestatus: not started\n- This is for cmd/pathchk/\n- Only R2.x requirements are ready, meaning R1.x requirements are either complete or not ready\n\n### srd104-test (8 ready requirements: R1.1, R1.2, R2.1, R2.2, R3.1, R3.2, R4.1, R4.2)\n- codestatus: not started\n- This is for cmd/test/\n\nWith the constraint that each task can have at most 4 requirements and total